      "So how should a farmer actually decide? The practical answer used by most agronomists is integrated nutrient management: use soil-test-based chemical fertilizer for precision and timing, and add organic matter every year for soil health. For example, apply well-rotted manure or compost before sowing to build the soil, then use the /calculator to work out the exact urea, DAP, and MOP dose your crop still needs on top of it.",
      "A few practical rules make the combination work. Compost manure before applying so nutrients release predictably and weed seeds die. Do not count manure as a full replacement for phosphorus and potassium unless you have lab analysis of that specific batch. Keep applying chemical fertilizer by the soil test, not by habit, and watch the field: darker, crumblier soil and better moisture retention over a few seasons are the signs your organic additions are paying off.",
