    "body": [
      "The best time of day to apply fertilizer is early morning or late in the evening. During these cooler hours the sun is low, the air is usually calm, and the soil is often damp from dew or the previous night. Those three conditions keep more of your fertilizer working for the plant and less of it lost to the air. The worst time, by contrast, is the middle of the day, when heat, bright sun, and wind all work against you.",
      "The main reason timing matters is a process called volatilization, and it mostly affects urea, the 46-0-0 fertilizer that most farmers use for nitrogen. When urea sits on a warm, moist soil surface, it converts into ammonia gas and drifts away. This happens fastest in hot, sunny, windy weather. A bag of urea spread at noon on a hot day can lose a meaningful share of its nitrogen before it ever reaches the roots, while the same bag spread in the cool of the morning loses far less.",
      "Heat speeds up every chemical reaction on the soil surface, and the conversion of urea to ammonia is no exception. Soil that has been baking in the midday sun is the worst possible landing spot for urea granules. In the morning the soil is cooler, so the conversion happens slowly, and that slow pace gives you time to water the granules in or let them dissolve into the soil on their own.",
      "Wind is the second enemy. Even on a warm day, still air lets ammonia linger close to the soil surface, where some of it can be recaptured. A steady breeze carries it away permanently. Mornings and evenings are generally calmer than midday, which is another reason the bookends of the day are better. If you can feel a strong wind on your face, it is not a good moment to spread urea.",
      "Morning dew is actually your friend. Granules that land on dew-damp soil start dissolving right away, and dissolved urea moves down into the soil with the first watering or light rain. Once urea is a few centimetres below the surface, it is protected from volatilization. This is why many experienced farmers spread fertilizer at first light: the dew does half the incorporation work for free.",
      "The single most effective thing you can do after spreading is to water the fertilizer in. A light irrigation, or timing your application just before a gentle rain, washes the granules into the root zone where they belong. If you spread in the morning, you can irrigate the same day and finish the job. If you spread at noon and cannot water until evening, the hottest hours are lost to the air.",
      "Here is a simple routine that works in most situations. First, check the weather forecast and pick a day with light winds and no heavy rain expected. Second, start early in the morning, ideally within a couple of hours of sunrise. Third, spread evenly and at the correct dose, because timing cannot fix over-application. Fourth, irrigate lightly the same morning so the nutrients move into the soil.",
      "If morning is impossible and you must work at midday, you can still reduce losses. Incorporate the fertilizer into the soil with a light hoeing or harrowing instead of leaving it on the surface, or irrigate immediately after spreading. For liquid fertilizers the same logic applies, and it applies even more strongly, because a fine spray dries on hot leaf surfaces within minutes and can scorch them.",
      "Foliar sprays, where fertilizer is sprayed directly onto leaves, follow the same timing rule for a different reason. Sprayed in the cool of the morning or evening, the droplets stay wet long enough for the leaves to absorb the nutrients. Sprayed at midday, they dry almost instantly, absorption stops, and concentrated droplets can burn the leaf tissue. Never do foliar feeding under a harsh midday sun.",
      "Timing matters more in hot seasons and hot climates than in cool ones. In the middle of summer the difference between a morning and a midday application can be large, while on a cool, overcast autumn day the penalty for imperfect timing is much smaller. Match your care to the conditions: the hotter and windier it is, the more strictly you should stick to morning or evening.",
      "The most common mistakes are easy to list. Spreading urea at noon in summer and leaving it on the surface is the classic one. Another is fertilizing right before a heavy downpour, which washes granules away instead of watering them in. A third is applying fertilizer to drought-stressed, wilted plants at midday, which adds salt stress to water stress. And a fourth is simply using the wrong dose and hoping good timing will compensate, which it will not.",
      "Evening application deserves a word of its own. Late evening, after the heat has faded, is nearly as good as early morning. The one caution is with foliar sprays: leaves that stay wet all night in humid weather can invite fungal problems, so for sprays, morning is the safer of the two. For granular fertilizer worked into the soil, morning and evening are equally fine.",
      "Different fertilizer types have slightly different sensitivities. Urea is the most timing-sensitive because of volatilization. DAP, the 18-46-0 fertilizer, and MOP, the 0-0-60 fertilizer, are less affected by surface losses, but they still benefit from being watered in so the nutrients reach the root zone instead of sitting on top. The morning-or-evening habit is good practice for all of them.",
      "Getting the timing right is only half the job; the dose has to be right too. Use the dose calculator at /calculator to work out exactly how much urea, DAP, and MOP your crop and area need, browse fertilizer grades and what each one does at /fertilizers, and check /faq if you have questions about mixing products or application methods. Right dose, right time, right result."
    ]
